  Actually, let me reconsider the whole framing. The question asks which are MUST. If nearly everything is SHOULD, the answer is boring and arguably wrong: a memory system's guarantees must include "write it before the code" or there's no memory. I think a good answer has a clear spine: MUST = the properties that make memory *trustworthy and recoverable*: never lose a claim (K4.1/K4.3/W1.1), never store a secret (W3.2), always readable from a clone (Rd1.2), index in step (K6.2), provenance (W4.2), decision-before-code (W5.1/K1.1), session-start reads (Rd2.1), check before acting (Rd5.1). That's ~10-12 MUSTs. The rest SHOULD/guidance.

